      "problem": "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
